# Schema conventions

This Studio is standalone — it is the source of truth for the content
model. The Next.js app lives in a sibling folder (`voter-guide-app`) and
only fetches published content; it never defines or embeds schema. Do
not move this Studio into the app or add a Studio route there.

- `Entry` references `Race`; `Race` and `Measure` reference `Region`.
  Never add an array-of-references field going the other direction
  (e.g. `race.entries`) — this project deliberately uses one-directional
  references so there's exactly one place a relationship can break.
  **Exception:** `ballotRace` (see below) is an embedded object, not a
  document, so it has no `_id` an Entry could reference — it necessarily
  holds an `entries` reference array pointing *at* Entry documents. This
  is the one intentional inversion; don't generalize it back to `race`.
- `Region.tier` is a fixed 3-value enum (state/county/city) mapped to
  hardcoded top-level nav sections in the frontend. Don't make tier
  dynamic/editable.
- Rating and position option lists are locked: Entry rating is
  No Recommendation / Recommended / Endorsed; Measure position is
  Support / Oppose / No Position (field renamed from `recommendation` to
  `position` — see docs/backend-strategy.md §11). If asked to change
  either list, confirm with the user first — these went through a real
  stakeholder sign-off process, not just an engineering default.
  `position` and `reasoning` are required on both `measure` and
  `ballotMeasure` **when Content status is Published**. The write-up is
  one text field, not pros and cons, and the editor offers normal
  paragraphs only. `entry.reasoning` is required whenever a rating is
  set, including No Recommendation, and whenever Content status is
  Published. Draft and Pending items may leave rating, reasoning, or
  position empty so races can appear on the ballot before the write-up
  exists.
- `contentStatus` (`draft` | `pending` | `published`, default `pending`)
  is the public-guide workflow. Document types use `liveEdit: true` —
  there is no Sanity draft/publish copy, and saving writes through.
  Content-status Draft is excluded from the app's GROQ filter; pending
  (or a published item with no rating/position yet) renders as
  "Write-up coming soon"; published with a rating shows the badge.
  No Recommendation is a real rating, not an empty write-up. Existing
  production documents were backfilled by
  `migrations/backfill-content-status`. Don't treat a missing value as a
  live fallback — run the migration.
- City-tier ballot content (see docs/backend-strategy.md §11) uses a
  different pattern than State/County: a City-tier `region.sections`
  array holds `raceGroup`/`measureGroup` blocks, whose `ballotRace`/
  `ballotMeasure` items are embedded objects (ownership by containment),
  not documents. `specialDistrict` uses the identical `sections` shape
  for content spanning multiple cities, plus a `citiesServed`
  multi-reference. State/County keep using the original `race`/`measure`
  document model unchanged — don't collapse the two models into one
  without asking; they solve genuinely different problems (fixed slate
  vs. wildly variable ballots + multi-city districts).
- Every *document* type needs an `order` field for manual sort, since
  independent top-level documents returned by a GROQ filter have no
  inherent order. Arrays that already live inside one document
  (`sections`, `raceGroup.races`, `measureGroup.measures`) do **not** get
  an `order` field — Studio's array editor natively drag-reorders them,
  and GROQ returns array items in stored order. Don't add a field that
  duplicates that built-in behavior.
- `race.district` and `ballotRace.district` are optional string fields
  holding a machine-matchable district code (e.g. `"CD4"`, `"SD24"`,
  `"CC4"`) for address-based ballot matching — see
  docs/address-matching-strategy.md and docs/backend-strategy.md §12.
  Blank means at-large/citywide, and such races always render once their
  parent Region matches regardless of match precision. This is a *display
  string doubling as a match key* — free text, not a reference or enum —
  so don't add validation constraining its format without asking; the
  prefix convention is documented, not enforced.
- Don't add fields not grounded in the PRD without asking. This project
  has a history of scope creep getting pushed back on; keep the schema
  lean.
- After changing schema here, run `npx sanity schemas deploy` so the
  Next.js app's GROQ query and any MCP tooling see the update.
